Slide 1 – FAILURE (~2 min)

“Failure.

This single word carries a lot of weight in our world of production support.

How many of you have been woken up at 2 a.m. for a bridgeline call because a critical job failed?
(Look around the room, raise your hand, pause, smile)
Yes… quite a few hands. I’ve been there too.

Failures don’t just stop systems.
They stop our business processes,
delay trade settlements,
and sometimes trigger major incidents that reach senior leadership.

It’s like driving on a busy highway and suddenly getting a flat tire.
But here, it’s not just one car that stops — it’s global traffic coming to a halt.

Today, we’ll explore one simple idea:
What if we could predict that flat tire before it happens?
What if failures didn’t have to surprise us anymore?”

Slide 2 – Agenda (~1.5 min)

“Here’s our journey for today.

First, we’ll talk about the problem — why job failures happen and why they hurt so much.

Next, the cost — how these failures impact our teams, our SLAs, and even KPAs like stability and availability.

Then, we’ll see the shift — moving from firefighting on bridgelines to proactively preventing failures.

After that, the framework — a step-by-step approach using AI and tools we already have today.

And finally, real-world cases — showing how this works in our production environment.”

Slide 3 – Pain Points of Traditional Monitoring (~3 min)

“Let’s start with how things work today.

Traditional monitoring is mostly reactive and very manual.
When a job fails, here’s what usually happens:

A bridgeline opens,

We scroll through logs,

We raise tickets,

We rerun jobs,

And repeat the same fixes again and again.

First problem — Manual log checks.
Imagine being on a bridgeline at 3 a.m., everyone staring at different log files trying to figure out what went wrong.
It’s slow, it’s tiring, and it’s repetitive.

Second — Delayed detection.
Sometimes, by the time we even know there’s a failure,
the impact has already spread to other jobs,
and SLA breaches are almost guaranteed.

Third — Repetitive fixes.
We see the same failures happening week after week.
Each time, we fix them manually.
It’s like running on a treadmill — lots of effort, no real progress.

Finally — stress and burnout.
This constant firefighting wears teams down.
Too many late-night calls, too many escalations, too little rest.

Traditional monitoring doesn’t just waste time — it burns out people.”

Slide 4 – Time Wasted: The Impact (~3 min)

“Now let’s look at the numbers.

Industry studies show that 40 to 50% of developer and support time goes into debugging and fixing failures.

That’s six months of every year, spent not on innovation — but just firefighting.

Imagine hiring a team of top chefs…
But instead of cooking meals, half of them spend their day washing dishes.
That’s what’s happening in many production teams today.

For companies, this means missed SLAs, unhappy stakeholders, and millions lost in productivity.
For teams, it means frustration, escalations, and KPAs moving in the wrong direction.

Now imagine if we could cut that wasted time in half.
Three extra months per person, every year — focused on automation and improvements instead of firefighting.”

Slide 5 – From Reactive → To Proactive (~2 min)

“Here’s where we need to shift our approach.

Reactive monitoring is like fixing a bridge after it collapses.
Proactive monitoring is like having sensors that detect cracks early, so you fix them before there’s a disaster.

Right now, most of us live reactively:

Job fails,

Bridgeline opens,

Teams investigate,

Tickets get raised,

Jobs rerun,

Escalations happen.

Stress goes up, SLAs are missed, and business impact grows.

In a proactive world:

Systems learn from history,

Detect problems early,

And in many cases, auto-fix routine failures before a bridgeline is even needed.

This is the shift —
From constant firefighting → to stability and reliability.”

Slide 6 – Proactive AI/ML Framework & Tools (~6 min)

“Let’s walk through how we can actually make this work.
The good news?
We don’t need to reinvent the wheel — we can do this using tools we already have today.

Step 1 – Data Ingestion

We start by collecting data from everywhere: logs, job statuses, error messages, metrics.

Right now, this information is scattered across multiple servers.
That makes troubleshooting slow, especially during a bridgeline.

With Kafka, Splunk, and ElasticSearch,
we stream all this data into one place.
Historical data from Oracle and Vertica helps us track patterns over time.

This gives us real-time visibility into what’s happening.

Step 2 – Model Training

Once we have the data, we train machine learning models to understand it.

Using Python, TensorFlow, and PyTorch,
we teach the system:

What “normal” job behavior looks like,

And what patterns usually lead to failures or SLA breaches.

Over time, the model gets smarter,
learning from every incident and failure.

Step 3 – Real-Time Analysis

Next, we monitor jobs live as they run.

Tools like Spark Streaming or Flink compare current data to the model.
If something looks wrong — like a job running far slower than usual —
it’s flagged before it fails.

This early warning helps us act before downstream jobs are impacted.

Step 4 – Automated Response

Detection alone isn’t enough — we need to take action automatically for common, known issues.

With AutoSys, Ansible, or Python scripts, we can:

Restart jobs instantly,

Clear temp space,

Adjust parameters,

Or rerun with corrections.

This removes the need for a bridgeline for standard failures.
Recovery time goes from 30 minutes to just seconds.

Step 5 – Reporting & Dashboards

Finally, we need clear visibility for leadership and teams.

With Grafana, Kibana, and Tableau,
we can show:

What failures were auto-fixed,

Where human action was needed,

And SLA trends across regions.

This data also feeds back into the model,
so the system continuously improves over time.

This five-step framework helps us predict, prevent, and respond to failures —
reducing incidents, stabilizing SLAs, and cutting down bridgelines.”

Slide 7 – Real-World Use Cases (~3 min)

“Now let’s look at two real production issues where this approach makes a big difference.

Case 1 – Unique Constraint Error

A job fails because of duplicate or invalid records hitting a unique constraint.

The problem?
We don’t know how many bad records are in there.
So we fix one, rerun the job, fix another, rerun again…
It’s slow, frustrating, and painful — especially during EOD critical runs.

Proactive approach:
The system checks the data before the job starts.
It detects all bad records at once:

Either auto-cleans them, or

Sends a ready-to-use cleanup list to us.

This prevents repeated failures and unnecessary reruns.

Case 2 – Incorrect File Structure

Sometimes, upstream sends a file with extra columns or unexpected changes after a release.
Our downstream jobs aren’t ready for it, so they fail immediately —
causing a chain reaction of failures and a major incident.

Proactive approach:
The system validates file structure before processing.
If something looks wrong:

It auto-adjusts or ignores the extra columns, or

Alerts teams immediately to fix it before downstream jobs fail.

Both of these cases are common in production.
With proactive detection,
we save hours of manual cleanup,
avoid SLA breaches,
and reduce escalations.”

Slide 8 – Closing & Call to Action (~3 min)

“Let’s go back to where we started — failure.

We’ve all experienced it:

Skipped meals,

Late nights on bridgelines,

Stress when KPAs slip and SLAs are missed.

But today, I want to leave you with a different word: opportunity.

The opportunity to:

Prevent incidents instead of just reacting,

Reduce manual effort,

Stabilize our environment,

And give our teams more time to focus on improvements.

Firefighters are heroes.
But their ultimate goal isn’t to fight more fires —
it’s to stop fires from starting in the first place.

That’s our goal too.
With proactive failure management,
failures become predictable, manageable, and far less painful.

Thank you.”

